1
00:00:04,359 --> 00:00:09,558
After two or three days, when I had established myself in my room and had gone backwards and

2
00:00:09,559 --> 00:00:16,199
forwards to London several times, and had ordered all I wanted of my tradesmen, Mr. Pocket and I had

3
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:23,000
a long talk together. He knew more of my intended career than I knew myself, for he referred to his

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,080
having been told by Mr. Jaggers that I was not designed for any profession, and that I should

5
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:33,278
be well enough educated for my destiny if I could “hold my own” with the average of young

6
00:00:33,278 --> 00:00:40,118
men in prosperous circumstances. I acquiesced, of course, knowing nothing to the contrary.

7
00:00:40,118 --> 00:00:45,359
He advised my attending certain places in London, for the acquisition of such mere rudiments as I

8
00:00:45,359 --> 00:00:51,797
wanted, and my investing him with the functions of explainer and director of all my studies. He

9
00:00:51,798 --> 00:00:57,000
hoped that with intelligent assistance I should meet with little to discourage me, and should soon

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,199
be able to dispense with any aid but his. Through his way of saying this, and much more to similar

11
00:01:04,199 --> 00:01:10,679
purpose, he placed himself on confidential terms with me in an admirable manner; and I may state at

12
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:16,080
once that he was always so zealous and honourable in fulfilling his compact with me, that he made me

13
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:22,638
zealous and honourable in fulfilling mine with him. If he had shown indifference as a master,

14
00:01:22,638 --> 00:01:28,599
I have no doubt I should have returned the compliment as a pupil; he gave me no such excuse,

15
00:01:28,599 --> 00:01:34,438
and each of us did the other justice. Nor did I ever regard him as having anything ludicrous about

16
00:01:34,438 --> 00:01:41,519
him—or anything but what was serious, honest, and good—in his tutor communication with me.

17
00:01:42,159 --> 00:01:47,919
When these points were settled, and so far carried out as that I had begun to work in earnest,

18
00:01:47,919 --> 00:01:52,599
it occurred to me that if I could retain my bedroom in Barnard’s Inn, my life would be

19
00:01:52,599 --> 00:01:59,719
agreeably varied, while my manners would be none the worse for Herbert’s society. Mr. Pocket did

20
00:01:59,719 --> 00:02:05,240
not object to this arrangement, but urged that before any step could possibly be taken in it,

21
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:11,840
it must be submitted to my guardian. I felt that this delicacy arose out of the consideration

22
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:14,840
that the plan would save Herbert some expense,

23
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:19,638
so I went off to Little Britain and imparted my wish to Mr. Jaggers.

24
00:02:19,638 --> 00:02:22,520
“If I could buy the furniture now hired for me,” said I,

25
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:26,879
“and one or two other little things, I should be quite at home there.”

26
00:02:26,879 --> 00:02:28,398
“Go it!” said Mr. Jaggers,

27
00:02:28,398 --> 00:02:34,398
with a short laugh. “I told you you’d get on. Well! How much do you want?”

28
00:02:34,398 --> 00:02:37,039
I said I didn’t know how much.

29
00:02:37,039 --> 00:02:42,199
“Come!” retorted Mr. Jaggers. “How much? Fifty pounds?”

30
00:02:42,199 --> 00:02:44,598
“O, not nearly so much.”

31
00:02:44,599 --> 00:02:48,039
“Five pounds?” said Mr. Jaggers.

32
00:02:48,039 --> 00:02:52,958
This was such a great fall, that I said in discomfiture, “O, more than that.”

33
00:02:52,958 --> 00:02:58,199
“More than that, eh!” retorted Mr. Jaggers, lying in wait for me, with his hands in his pockets,

34
00:02:58,199 --> 00:03:04,039
his head on one side, and his eyes on the wall behind me; “how much more?”

35
00:03:04,039 --> 00:03:08,118
“It is so difficult to fix a sum,” said I, hesitating.

36
00:03:08,118 --> 00:03:12,317
“Come!” said Mr. Jaggers. “Let’s get at it. Twice five;

37
00:03:12,317 --> 00:03:18,159
will that do? Three times five; will that do? Four times five; will that do?”

38
00:03:18,159 --> 00:03:21,039
I said I thought that would do handsomely.

39
00:03:21,039 --> 00:03:24,477
“Four times five will do handsomely, will it?” said Mr. Jaggers,

40
00:03:24,478 --> 00:03:28,559
knitting his brows. “Now, what do you make of four times five?”

41
00:03:28,559 --> 00:03:30,478
“What do I make of it?”

42
00:03:30,478 --> 00:03:34,240
“Ah!” said Mr. Jaggers; “how much?”

43
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:38,398
“I suppose you make it twenty pounds,” said I, smiling.

44
00:03:38,398 --> 00:03:41,359
“Never mind what I make it, my friend,” observed Mr. Jaggers,

45
00:03:42,039 --> 00:03:48,380
with a knowing and contradictory toss of his head. “I want to know what you make it.”

46
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:50,000
“Twenty pounds, of course.”

47
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,080
“Wemmick!” said Mr. Jaggers,

48
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:59,477
opening his office door. “Take Mr. Pip’s written order, and pay him twenty pounds.”

49
00:03:59,478 --> 00:04:05,278
This strongly marked way of doing business made a strongly marked impression on me, and that not of

50
00:04:05,278 --> 00:04:13,798
an agreeable kind. Mr. Jaggers never laughed; but he wore great bright creaking boots, and,

51
00:04:13,798 --> 00:04:20,080
in poising himself on these boots, with his large head bent down and his eyebrows joined together,

52
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:25,519
awaiting an answer, he sometimes caused the boots to creak, as if they laughed in a dry

53
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:32,599
and suspicious way. As he happened to go out now, and as Wemmick was brisk and talkative,

54
00:04:32,599 --> 00:04:37,359
I said to Wemmick that I hardly knew what to make of Mr. Jaggers’s manner.

55
00:04:37,359 --> 00:04:41,317
“Tell him that, and he’ll take it as a compliment,” answered Wemmick;

56
00:04:41,317 --> 00:04:45,679
“he don’t mean that you should know what to make of it.—Oh!” for I looked

57
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:51,680
surprised, “it’s not personal; it’s professional: only professional.”

58
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:56,118
Wemmick was at his desk, lunching—and crunching—on a dry hard biscuit;

59
00:04:56,118 --> 00:05:02,438
pieces of which he threw from time to time into his slit of a mouth, as if he were posting them.

60
00:05:02,438 --> 00:05:04,718
“Always seems to me,” said Wemmick, “as if he

61
00:05:04,719 --> 00:05:10,199
had set a man-trap and was watching it. Suddenly—click—you’re caught!”

62
00:05:10,199 --> 00:05:13,958
Without remarking that man-traps were not among the amenities of life,

63
00:05:13,958 --> 00:05:21,599
I said I supposed he was very skilful? “Deep,” said Wemmick, “as Australia.”

64
00:05:21,599 --> 00:05:26,199
Pointing with his pen at the office floor, to express that Australia was understood,

65
00:05:26,199 --> 00:05:30,118
for the purposes of the figure, to be symmetrically on the opposite spot of

66
00:05:30,118 --> 00:05:38,000
the globe. “If there was anything deeper,” added Wemmick, bringing his pen to paper, “he’d be it.”

67
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,638
Then, I said I supposed he had a fine business, and Wemmick

68
00:05:41,638 --> 00:05:48,680
said, “Ca-pi-tal!” Then I asked if there were many clerks? to which he replied,—

69
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:53,118
“We don’t run much into clerks, because there’s only one Jaggers, and people won’t have him at

70
00:05:53,118 --> 00:06:01,477
second hand. There are only four of us. Would you like to see ’em? You are one of us, as I may say.”

71
00:06:01,478 --> 00:06:06,240
I accepted the offer. When Mr. Wemmick had put all the biscuit into the post,

72
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:11,558
and had paid me my money from a cash-box in a safe, the key of which safe he kept somewhere

73
00:06:11,559 --> 00:06:18,118
down his back and produced from his coat-collar like an iron-pigtail, we went upstairs. The house

74
00:06:18,118 --> 00:06:23,398
was dark and shabby, and the greasy shoulders that had left their mark in Mr. Jaggers’s room

75
00:06:23,398 --> 00:06:28,919
seemed to have been shuffling up and down the staircase for years. In the front first floor,

76
00:06:28,919 --> 00:06:33,359
a clerk who looked something between a publican and a rat-catcher—a large pale,

77
00:06:33,359 --> 00:06:39,080
puffed, swollen man—was attentively engaged with three or four people of shabby appearance, whom he

78
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:43,919
treated as unceremoniously as everybody seemed to be treated who contributed to Mr. Jaggers’s

79
00:06:43,919 --> 00:06:51,438
coffers. “Getting evidence together,” said Mr. Wemmick, as we came out, “for the Bailey.” In

80
00:06:51,438 --> 00:06:56,637
the room over that, a little flabby terrier of a clerk with dangling hair (his cropping seemed to

81
00:06:56,638 --> 00:07:02,000
have been forgotten when he was a puppy) was similarly engaged with a man with weak eyes,

82
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:08,000
whom Mr. Wemmick presented to me as a smelter who kept his pot always boiling, and who would melt me

83
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,680
anything I pleased,—and who was in an excessive white-perspiration, as if he had been trying his

84
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:21,759
art on himself. In a back room, a high-shouldered man with a face-ache tied up in dirty flannel,

85
00:07:21,759 --> 00:07:27,240
who was dressed in old black clothes that bore the appearance of having been waxed, was stooping over

86
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:34,080
his work of making fair copies of the notes of the other two gentlemen, for Mr. Jaggers’s own use.

87
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:38,879
This was all the establishment. When we went downstairs again,

88
00:07:38,879 --> 00:07:45,520
Wemmick led me into my guardian’s room, and said, “This you’ve seen already.”

89
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:49,080
“Pray,” said I, as the two odious casts with the

90
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:55,398
twitchy leer upon them caught my sight again, “whose likenesses are those?”

91
00:07:55,398 --> 00:08:00,797
“These?” said Wemmick, getting upon a chair, and blowing the dust off the horrible heads before

92
00:08:00,798 --> 00:08:07,398
bringing them down. “These are two celebrated ones. Famous clients of ours that got us a world

93
00:08:07,398 --> 00:08:13,958
of credit. This chap (why you must have come down in the night and been peeping into the inkstand,

94
00:08:13,958 --> 00:08:19,000
to get this blot upon your eyebrow, you old rascal!) murdered his master,

95
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:24,398
and, considering that he wasn’t brought up to evidence, didn’t plan it badly.”

96
00:08:24,398 --> 00:08:28,159
“Is it like him?” I asked, recoiling from the brute,

97
00:08:28,159 --> 00:08:33,039
as Wemmick spat upon his eyebrow and gave it a rub with his sleeve.

98
00:08:33,039 --> 00:08:38,599
“Like him? It’s himself, you know. The cast was made in Newgate, directly after he was

99
00:08:38,599 --> 00:08:46,240
taken down. You had a particular fancy for me, hadn’t you, Old Artful?” said Wemmick.

100
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:51,438
He then explained this affectionate apostrophe, by touching his brooch representing the lady and

101
00:08:51,438 --> 00:08:58,878
the weeping willow at the tomb with the urn upon it, and saying, “Had it made for me, express!”

102
00:08:58,879 --> 00:09:01,520
“Is the lady anybody?” said I.

103
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:07,919
“No,” returned Wemmick. “Only his game. (You liked your bit of game, didn’t you?) No;

104
00:09:07,919 --> 00:09:12,680
deuce a bit of a lady in the case, Mr. Pip, except one,—and she wasn’t of this

105
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:17,317
slender lady-like sort, and you wouldn’t have caught her looking after this urn,

106
00:09:17,317 --> 00:09:22,278
unless there was something to drink in it.” Wemmick’s attention being thus directed to his

107
00:09:22,278 --> 00:09:28,000
brooch, he put down the cast, and polished the brooch with his pocket-handkerchief.

108
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:33,680
“Did that other creature come to the same end?” I asked. “He has the same look.”

109
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:39,438
“You’re right,” said Wemmick; “it’s the genuine look. Much as if one nostril was caught up with a

110
00:09:39,438 --> 00:09:45,240
horse-hair and a little fish-hook. Yes, he came to the same end; quite the natural end here,

111
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:51,240
I assure you. He forged wills, this blade did, if he didn’t also put the supposed testators to

112
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:57,958
sleep too. You were a gentlemanly Cove, though” (Mr. Wemmick was again apostrophising), “and you

113
00:09:57,958 --> 00:10:05,080
said you could write Greek. Yah, Bounceable! What a liar you were! I never met such a liar

114
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:11,278
as you!” Before putting his late friend on his shelf again, Wemmick touched the largest

115
00:10:11,278 --> 00:10:17,558
of his mourning rings and said, “Sent out to buy it for me, only the day before.”

116
00:10:17,559 --> 00:10:21,240
While he was putting up the other cast and coming down from the chair,

117
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:27,637
the thought crossed my mind that all his personal jewelry was derived from like sources. As he had

118
00:10:27,638 --> 00:10:32,638
shown no diffidence on the subject, I ventured on the liberty of asking him the question,

119
00:10:32,638 --> 00:10:40,520
when he stood before me, dusting his hands. “O yes,” he returned, “these are all gifts

120
00:10:40,520 --> 00:10:47,599
of that kind. One brings another, you see; that’s the way of it. I always take ’em.

121
00:10:47,599 --> 00:10:53,240
They’re curiosities. And they’re property. They may not be worth much, but, after all,

122
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:58,240
they’re property and portable. It don’t signify to you with your brilliant lookout,

123
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:04,398
but as to myself, my guiding-star always is, ‘Get hold of portable property’.”

124
00:11:04,398 --> 00:11:09,438
When I had rendered homage to this light, he went on to say, in a friendly manner:—

125
00:11:09,438 --> 00:11:13,359
“If at any odd time when you have nothing better to do, you wouldn’t mind coming over

126
00:11:13,359 --> 00:11:19,039
to see me at Walworth, I could offer you a bed, and I should consider it an honour. I

127
00:11:19,039 --> 00:11:24,519
have not much to show you; but such two or three curiosities as I have got you

128
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:30,638
might like to look over; and I am fond of a bit of garden and a summer-house.”

129
00:11:30,638 --> 00:11:34,240
I said I should be delighted to accept his hospitality.

130
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:38,240
“Thankee,” said he; “then we’ll consider that it’s to come off,

131
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:42,919
when convenient to you. Have you dined with Mr. Jaggers yet?”

132
00:11:42,919 --> 00:11:44,520
“Not yet.”

133
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:52,559
“Well,” said Wemmick, “he’ll give you wine, and good wine. I’ll give you punch, and not bad punch.

134
00:11:52,559 --> 00:11:59,199
And now I’ll tell you something. When you go to dine with Mr. Jaggers, look at his housekeeper.”

135
00:11:59,199 --> 00:12:02,317
“Shall I see something very uncommon?”

136
00:12:02,317 --> 00:12:07,477
“Well,” said Wemmick, “you’ll see a wild beast tamed. Not so very uncommon,

137
00:12:07,478 --> 00:12:13,278
you’ll tell me. I reply, that depends on the original wildness of the beast, and the amount

138
00:12:13,278 --> 00:12:20,637
of taming. It won’t lower your opinion of Mr. Jaggers’s powers. Keep your eye on it.”

139
00:12:20,638 --> 00:12:24,317
I told him I would do so, with all the interest and curiosity

140
00:12:24,317 --> 00:12:28,958
that his preparation awakened. As I was taking my departure,

141
00:12:28,958 --> 00:12:34,359
he asked me if I would like to devote five minutes to seeing Mr. Jaggers “at it?”

142
00:12:34,359 --> 00:12:38,118
For several reasons, and not least because I didn’t clearly know what Mr. Jaggers would

143
00:12:38,118 --> 00:12:43,638
be found to be “at,” I replied in the affirmative. We dived into the City,

144
00:12:43,638 --> 00:12:48,559
and came up in a crowded police-court, where a blood-relation (in the murderous sense) of the

145
00:12:48,559 --> 00:12:54,199
deceased, with the fanciful taste in brooches, was standing at the bar, uncomfortably chewing

146
00:12:54,199 --> 00:12:59,759
something; while my guardian had a woman under examination or cross-examination,—I don’t know

147
00:12:59,759 --> 00:13:05,958
which,—and was striking her, and the bench, and everybody present, with awe. If anybody,

148
00:13:05,958 --> 00:13:10,840
of whatsoever degree, said a word that he didn’t approve of, he instantly required to

149
00:13:10,840 --> 00:13:17,317
have it “taken down.” If anybody wouldn’t make an admission, he said, “I’ll have it out of you!” and

150
00:13:17,317 --> 00:13:24,118
if anybody made an admission, he said, “Now I have got you!” The magistrates shivered under a single

151
00:13:24,118 --> 00:13:30,840
bite of his finger. Thieves and thief-takers hung in dread rapture on his words, and shrank when a

152
00:13:30,840 --> 00:13:36,638
hair of his eyebrows turned in their direction. Which side he was on I couldn’t make out, for he

153
00:13:36,638 --> 00:13:42,398
seemed to me to be grinding the whole place in a mill; I only know that when I stole out on tiptoe,

154
00:13:42,398 --> 00:13:47,438
he was not on the side of the bench; for, he was making the legs of the old gentleman who presided,

155
00:13:47,438 --> 00:13:52,359
quite convulsive under the table, by his denunciations of his conduct as the representative

156
00:13:52,359 --> 00:13:55,000
of British law and justice in that chair that day. Chapter 25.
